    full: `The Ik-Rasul
No one claims it cleanly.
Solmaran and Hoshimiran reports describe it as a weapon. A Sahrani construct, built in secret, launched with intent. The language in those documents is precise and confident as if it has already decided what happened and is working backward toward evidence. No Sahrani record acknowledges the plane's existence. No builder is named. No commission, no materials requisition, no account of the years it would have taken to assemble something capable of crossing the distance it crossed.
What the reports agree on is the wreckage. Fire Gems still burning in the engine casing when it came down. Ankaru crystals shattered across the Aokusa fields, the cold bleeding out of them into soil that didn't know what to do with it. The frame was scavenged work, mech parts from different eras, different origins, fitted together with the kind of patience that doesn't answer to deadlines or oversight. Whoever built it had time. Whoever built it had somewhere to build it that no one was looking.
The old Autarch structures in the upper Geode, the administrative tiers built during their first claim on the place, sat largely unexamined after the rebellion. Too high up to be useful, too embedded to be easily cleared. Cavities in the rock. Scaffolding that predated the councils. Spaces where a project could breathe across years without becoming visible.
The story told in certain Sahrani communities, quietly and without much insistence on its truth, involves a boy and his father. The details shift depending on who is telling it. What stays consistent is the destination. They were flying back to Aokusa. Back to the mountain. Back to whatever the mountain meant to people who had spent generations praying toward it from the desert.
They called the plane Ik-Rasul. In the old tongue, something close to I am the Apostle. A name that carries its own verdict depending on your faith. Devotion or arrogance. Pilgrimage or transgression. The sun found the engines before they found the mountain, and the plane came down into the fields it was trying to reach.
Whether this is history or the shape history takes when it wants to be forgiven, I have never been able to say.
The fields remember the cold of the Ankaru. The aquifers remember whatever came after. The Geode, as always, says nothing.`,
